ue. This reasoning, however, is not so readily applicable when it comes to the bargaining model proposed by Nash in the early 1950s. One discrepancy is, for instance, the removal of the time aspect that is a feature of the model, which greatly differs from the standard economic approach to bargaining. Moreover, and more generally, the set of axioms proposed by Nash to determine a solution to the bargaining Correspondence may be addressed to Alessandro Innocenti, Department of Economic Policy,"}]", "excerpt_truncated": true, "source_sha256": "dea4301e7df6f9327f91e2eea6d9bc3406d930469b34b414572899c24d0d9e6b", "verification_required": true, "topic_domain": "assumption_challenge", "evidence_role": "discovery", "host_tier": "discovery", "source_identity": null, "persistent_identifiers": ["doi:10.70675/ac07ff5dz534bz421fz89ddz67ecc1559377", "openalex:W4392150277", "openalex:W3125346028"], "source_candidates": ["https://doi.org/10.70675/ac07ff5dz534bz421fz89ddz67ecc1559377", "https://theses.hal.science/tel-03406784v1/file/2021COAZ4052.pdf", "https://hal.science/tel-03406784v1"], "source_candidate_identities": {}}
